s="\"Restricted Matchings (https://d-nb.info/1190178249/34)\\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; asymptotically for large girth, the uniquely restricted matching number behaves like the ... Conjecture 6.4 ([37]).\\n6 Lower Bounds on the Uniquely Restricted Matching Number\\nu(1)\\nu(2)\\nu(k)\\nK(1)\\nK(2)\\nK(3)\\nK(4)\\nK(5)\\nK(2k −1)\\nK(2k)\\nK(2k + 1)\\nFigure 6.1: An illustration of the graph Gk. The dashed edges illustrate a maximum\\nuniquely restricted matching.\\nTheorem 6.3 ([37]). If G is a connected graph of maximum degree at most ∆for some\\n∆≥4 and girth at least 5, then\\nνur(G) ≥n(G) −1\\n∆\\n.\\n(6.2)\\nAgain, (6.2) is tight for inﬁnitely many trees [49]. In the next two chapters, we will\\nsee that (6.2) essentially also holds for subcubic graphs.\\nNote that the bound (6.2) is also a best-possible lower bound for the ordinary matching\\nnumber [49]. This suggests to study the ratio νur(G)/ν(G). We believe that, at least\\nasymptotically for large girth, the uniquely restricted matching number behaves like the\\nmatching number.\\nConjecture 6.4 ([37]). For every positive ϵ and every positive integer ∆, there is some\\npositive integer g = g(ϵ, ∆), such that νur(G) ≥(1 −ϵ)ν(G) for every graph G of\\nmaximum degree at most ∆and girth at least g.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCycles as edge intersection hypergraphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00396)\\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Title: Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs ... Picking up a problem from arXiv:1901.06292, for $n \\\\ge 24$ we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if $n$ is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph ${\\\\cal H}=(V,{\\\\cal E})$ with $\\\\lceil \\\\frac{n}{2} \\\\rceil$ hyperedges and $EI({\\\\cal H}) = C_n$.\\nTitle: Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs\\nAuthors: Martin Sonntag, Hanns-Martin Teichert\\nDate: Fri Feb  1 15:18:08 2019\\n\\nIf ${\\\\cal H}=(V,{\\\\cal E})$ is a hypergraph, its edge intersection hypergraph $EI({\\\\cal H})=(V,{\\\\cal E}^{EI})$ has the edge set ${\\\\cal E}^{EI}=\\\\{e_1 \\\\cap e_2 \\\\ |\\\\ e_1, e_2 \\\\in {\\\\cal E} \\\\ \\\\wedge \\\\ e_1 \\\\neq e_2 \\\\ \\\\wedge \\\\ |e_1 \\\\cap e_2 |\\\\geq2\\\\}$. Picking up a problem from arXiv:1901.06292, for $n \\\\ge 24$ we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if $n$ is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph ${\\\\cal H}=(V,{\\\\cal E})$ with $\\\\lceil \\\\frac{n}{2} \\\\rceil$ hyperedges and $EI({\\\\cal H}) = C_n$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nHigh-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/338806/high-girth-approximation-conjecture-for-uniquely-restricted)\\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; High-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number\\n\\nHigh-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number\\n\\nAbout 8 years old · traced to 2018\\n\\nFor a graph $G$G, let $\\\\nu(G)$ν(G) denote its matching number and $\\\\nu_{ur}(G)$ν u r​(G) its uniquely restricted matching number. For every positive real number $\\\\epsilon$ϵ and every positive integer $\\\\Delta$Δ, consider graphs of maximum degree at most $\\\\Delta$Δ and girth at least $g$g. High-girth approximation conjecture. There is some positive integer $g=g(\\\\epsilon,\\\\Delta)$g=g(ϵ,Δ) such that\\n\\n$$\\\\nu_{ur}(G)\\\\geq (1-\\\\epsilon)\\\\nu(G)$$ν u r​(G)≥(1−ϵ)ν(G)\\n\\nfor every graph $G$G of maximum degree at most $\\\\Delta$Δ and girth at least $g$g. The conjecture asserts that, for fixed maximum degree, sufficiently large girth makes the uniquely restricted matching number asymptotically as close as desired to the ordinary matching number; the source gives no resolution, so it remains open.\\n\\n## Primary source\\n\\nM. Fürst and D. Rautenbach, “Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number”, arXiv:1803.11032 (2018).\\n\\ngraph-theoryhigh-girth-graphsmatchings\\n\\n0 0\\n\\n3 weeks ago\\n\\n## Progress summary\\n\\nRefresh Never refreshed\\n\\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\\n\\n## Solutions 0--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCycles as edge intersection hypergraphs of $k$-uniform hypergraphs ($k \\\\le 6$) -- a constructive approach (https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.06245)\\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Title: Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs of $k$-uniform hypergraphs ($k \\\\le 6$) -- a constructive approach\\nTitle: Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs of $k$-uniform hypergraphs ($k \\\\le 6$) -- a constructive approach\\nAuthors: Sophie Pätz, Martin Sonntag\\nDate: Fri Nov 11 14:36:37 2022\\n\\nIf ${\\\\cal H}=(V,{\\\\cal E})$ is a hypergraph, its edge intersection hypergraph $EI({\\\\cal H})=(V,{\\\\cal E}^{EI})$ has the edge set ${\\\\cal E}^{EI}=\\\\{e_1 \\\\cap e_2 \\\\ |\\\\ e_1, e_2 \\\\in {\\\\cal E} \\\\ \\\\wedge \\\\ e_1 \\\\neq e_2 \\\\ \\\\wedge \\\\ |e_1 \\\\cap e_2 |\\\\geq 2\\\\}$. In the present paper, we consider 4- and 5-uniform hypergraphs ${\\\\cal H}$, respectively, with $EI({\\\\cal H}) = C_n$. Our results fill the gap between the 3- and the 6-uniform case considered in arXiv:1902.00396.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCycles as edge intersection hypergraphs (https://research.uni-luebeck.de/en/publications/cycles-as-edge-intersection-hypergraphs/)\\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; # Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs ... Pickingupaproblem from [7], for n ≥ 24 we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if n is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph H = (V,E) with ⌈n⌉ hyperedges and EI(H) = Cn.\\n\\n# Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs\\n\\n  * Martin Sonntag\\n  * , Hanns-Martin Teichert\\n\\n  * Institute of Mathematics\\n\\n  * Technische Universität Bergakademie Freiberg\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nIf H = (V,E) is a hypergraph, its edge intersection hypergraph EI(H) = (V,EEI) hastheedgesetEEI={e1∩e2|e1,e2∈E∧e1̸=e2 ∧|e1∩e2|≥2}.Pickingupaproblem from [7], for n ≥ 24 we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if n is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph H = (V,E) with ⌈n⌉ hyperedges and EI(H) = Cn.\\n\\nOriginal language  | English\\nPublication status  | Published - 2019\\n\\n## Access to Document\\n\\n  * https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00396\\n\\nSonntag, M., & Teichert, H.-M. (2019). Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs. https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00396\\n\\nSonntag, Martin ; Teichert, Hanns-Martin. / Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs. 2019. 21 p.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.00840)\\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.5 years ago; Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order $n$ and girth at least $7$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of F\\\\\\\"{u}rst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).\\nTitle: Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs\\nAuthors: Maximilian Fürst, Michael A. Henning, Dieter Rautenbach\\nDate: Wed May  2 14:20:04 2018\\n\\nA matching $M$ in a graph $G$ is uniquely restricted if no other matching in $G$ covers the same set of vertices. We conjecture that every connected subcubic graph with $m$ edges and $b$ bridges that is distinct from $K_{3,3}$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\\\frac{m+b}{6}$, and we establish this bound with $b$ replaced by the number of bridges that lie on a path between two vertices of degree at most $2$. Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order $n$ and girth at least $7$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of F\\\\\\\"{u}rst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs (https://pure.uj.ac.za/en/publications/uniquely-restricted-matchings-in-subcubic-graphs/)\\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order n and girth at least 7 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least n/−13, which partially confirms a conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Graphs Combin. ... Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order n and girth at least 7 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least n/−13, which partially confirms a conjecture of F{\\\\\\\"u}rst and Rautenbach (Graphs Combin.\\n\\n# Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs\\n\\n  * Maximilian Fürst\\n  * , Michael A. Henning\\n  * , Dieter Rautenbach\\n\\n  * Mathematics and Applied Mathematics\\n\\n  * Ulm University\\n\\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\\n\\n8 Citations (Scopus)\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nA matching M in a graph G is uniquely restricted if no other matching in G covers the same set of vertices. We conjecture that every connected subcubic graph with m edges and b bridges that is distinct from K _{3,3} has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least m/+b6, and we establish this bound with b replaced by the number of bridges that lie on a path between two vertices of degree at most 2. Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order n and girth at least 7 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least n/−13, which partially confirms a conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Graphs Combin. 35 (2019) 353–361).\\n\\nOriginal