 that every connected subcubic graph of order n and girth at least 7 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least n/−13, which partially confirms a conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Graphs Combin. 35 (2019) 353–361).\\n\\nJO - Discrete Applied Mathematics\\n\\nJF - Discrete Applied Mathematics\\n\\nER -\\n\\nFürst M, Henning MA, Rautenbach D. Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs. Discrete Applied Mathematics. 2019 Jun 15;262:189-194. doi: 10.1016/j.dam.2019.02.013\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/jgt.22632)\\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles ... We prove that any connected subcubic graph with 𝑛n vertices and girth at least 5 contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least (𝑛−1)/3\\\\left(n-1\\\\right)/3 except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order 14 and 20. ... Plummer, Matching theory, North-Holland, Amsterdam, 1986.\\n\\nCorrespondence D. Rautenbach, Institute of Optimization and Operations Research, Ulm University, Ulm, Germany.\\n\\nEmail: dieter.rautenbach@uni-ulm.de\\n\\nFirst published: 19 October 2020\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.22632Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\\n\\nview metrics\\n\\nRead the full text\\n\\n  * References\\n\\n### Abstract\\n\\nA matching 𝑀M in a graph 𝐺G is uniquely restricted if no other matching in 𝐺G covers the same set of vertices. We prove that any connected subcubic graph with 𝑛n vertices and girth at least 5 contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least (𝑛−1)/3\\\\left(n-1\\\\right)/3 except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order 14 and 20.\\n\\nREFERENCES\\n\\n  * 1 V. Costa, S. Dantas, and D. Rautenbach, Matchings in graphs of odd regularity and girth, Discrete Math. 313 (2013), 2895–2902.\\n\\nWeb of Science® Google Scholar\\n\\n  * 2 A. D. Flaxman and S. Hoory, Maximum matchings in regular graphs of high girth, Electron. J. Combinatorics 14 (2007), #N1.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/1805.00840v1)\\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order $n$ and girth at least $7$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).\\n\\n## Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs\\n\\nMaximilian Fürst, Michael A. Henning, Dieter Rautenbach\\n\\nPublished: 2018-05-02\\n\\n\\ngraph matching\\n\\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\\n\\n### Abstract\\n\\nA matching $M$ in a graph $G$ is uniquely restricted if no other matching in $G$ covers the same set of vertices. We conjecture that every connected subcubic graph with $m$ edges and $b$ bridges that is distinct from $K_{3,3}$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\\\frac{m+b}{6}$, and we establish this bound with $b$ replaced by the number of bridges that lie on a path between two vertices of degree at most $2$. Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order $n$ and girth at least $7$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).\\n\\n### BibTeX\\n\\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.